{"name":"Crystal River","hex":"#b1e2ee","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Crystal River","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The name of several rivers in the United States.\nA tributary of the Roaring Fork River in Gunnison County, Garfield County and Pitkin County, Colorado.\nA river in Citrus County, Florida, flowing into the Gulf of Mexico.\nA river in Leelanau County, Michigan, flowing into Lake Michigan.\nA tributary of the Tomorrow/Waupaca River in Waupaca County, Wisconsin.","A tributary of the Roaring Fork River in Gunnison County, Garfield County and Pitkin County, Colorado."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Crystal_River"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crystal River, Florida","blocks":[{"text":"Crystal River is a city in Citrus County, Florida, United States. The population was 3,396 in the 2020 census, up from 3,108 at the 2010 census."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/NB_US_19-98_Crystal_River_Sign.JPG/330px-NB_US_19-98_Crystal_River_Sign.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_River%2C_Florida","wikidata":"Q994967"},{"title":"Crystal River (Florida)","blocks":[{"text":"Crystal River is a river in Citrus County, Florida, flowing into the Gulf of Mexico. It is just seven miles long, and has a drainage basin of five square miles, joining Kings Bay to the Gulf of Mexico. The river's significance is in the thirty natural springs that add an average of 300 million gallons of warm water to the river every day. These springs include Three Sisters Springs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Crystal_River_Arch_Park_TM05.jpg/330px-Crystal_River_Arch_Park_TM05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_River_(Florida)","wikidata":"Q5191334"},{"title":"Crystal","blocks":[{"text":"A crystal or crystalline solid is a solid material whose constituents are arranged in a highly ordered microscopic structure, forming a crystal lattice that extends in all directions. In addition, macroscopic single crystals are usually identifiable by their geometrical shape, consisting of flat faces with specific, characteristic orientations. The scientific study of crystals and crystal formation is known as crystallography. The process of crystal formation via mechanisms of crystal growth is called crystallization or solidification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Quartz_28.jpg/330px-Quartz_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal","wikidata":"Q43533"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River","wikidata":"Q4022"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/cc/CrystalRiverFLseal.gif/120px-CrystalRiverFLseal.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/cc/CrystalRiverFLseal.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/cc/CrystalRiverFLseal.gif/120px-CrystalRiverFLseal.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrystalRiverFLseal.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Crwater.jpg/500px-Crwater.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Crwater.jpg/500px-Crwater.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crystal River's Water Tower","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrwater.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/KingsBay.jpg/500px-KingsBay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/KingsBay.jpg/500px-KingsBay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kings Bay is fed by a springs system; it is connected to the Gulf of Mexico by the Crystal River.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKingsBay.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/CRHS.jpeg/500px-CRHS.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/CRHS.jpeg/500px-CRHS.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crystal River High School","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACRHS.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png/500px-Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/So